1. Current WebSocket/STOMP Frontend Architecture

STOMP client library: @stomp/stompjs v7.0.0
Client class: Client from @stomp/stompjs

RealtimeService (frontend/src/app/realtime/realtime.service.ts) owns the STOMP client. It is constructed on demand in connect(), activated with client.activate(), and deactivated with client.deactivate().

MapComponent holds a reference to RealtimeService and calls connect() in ngAfterViewInit(), disconnect() in ngOnDestroy() and logout().

---
2. Current Reconnect Behavior

reconnectDelay: 3000 is configured. @stomp/stompjs reconnects automatically after 3 seconds whenever the underlying WebSocket closes unexpectedly. Automatic reconnect IS present and active.

The onConnect callback fires on every successful STOMP CONNECTED frame — both the initial connect and each reconnect. A firstConnect flag tracks whether this is the initial connect or a reconnect:

let firstConnect = true;
onConnect: () => {
  this.client!.subscribe(`/topic/workspace/${workspaceId}`, (message) => { ... });
  if (!firstConnect) {
    onReconnect();          // calls loadViewport() in MapComponent
  }
  firstConnect = false;
},

On reconnect, onReconnect() is called — which is () => this.loadViewport() in MapComponent. The reconnect path therefore triggers a full REST viewport reload. The core reconnect-and-reconcile flow is already wired.

---
3. Current Subscription Lifecycle

The subscription to /topic/workspace/{workspaceId} is created inside onConnect. Since onConnect fires on every successful STOMP session establishment (initial + reconnect), a new subscription is created each time.

There is no subscription accumulation. When the WebSocket session closes, the old subscription handle becomes stale (its underlying session is gone). The new subscription created in the next onConnect is the only live one. The backend SimpleBroker holds no subscriber state between sessions — each CONNECT starts a fresh STOMP session.

---
4. Current JWT Behavior During Reconnect

Gap identified.

connectHeaders is set once at construction time:

connectHeaders: {
  Authorization: `Bearer ${this.authService.getToken()}`,
},

The token is captured at the moment connect() is called (page load). On every automatic reconnect, @stomp/stompjs reuses the same connectHeaders object — it does not re-evaluate it. If the JWT has changed (user re-authenticated in another tab) or expired, the reconnect silently uses the stale token.

@stomp/stompjs v7 provides a beforeConnect callback that runs before each connection attempt. Assigning a new object to client.connectHeaders inside beforeConnect causes the new value to be used in the CONNECT frame. This is the correct fix.

---
5. Current REST Behavior When WebSocket Is Unavailable

REST operations are entirely independent of the WebSocket state. The HTTP authInterceptor (frontend/src/app/auth/auth.interceptor.ts) attaches the current token from localStorage to every REST request. There is no code that disables REST when WebSocket is disconnected.

All five mutation operations (create, update, delete, restore, get) use AreaService which issues plain HttpClient calls. Each success path directly updates the UI without waiting for any WebSocket event:
- createArea → addPersistedArea(area) on success
- updateArea → clearEdit(); addPersistedArea(area) on success
- deleteArea → adds gray polygon, sets deletedArea on success
- restoreArea → clearDeletedArea(); addPersistedArea(restored) on success

REST graceful degradation already works correctly. No gap here.

