Answers to Explicit Questions

A. Can the application currently reconnect automatically?
Yes. reconnectDelay: 3000 is configured and active. @stomp/stompjs reconnects after 3 seconds without any additional code.

B. Does reconnect currently guarantee state convergence?
Mostly. The onReconnect → loadViewport() path is wired and correct. The gap is that connectHeaders are not refreshed with the current JWT — reconnect uses the token from the original connect() call. This is fixed with beforeConnect.

C. How will a client recover events missed during disconnect?
Via loadViewport() called in onReconnect. It issues GET /workspaces/{id}/areas?bbox=<current-bounds>, which returns the authoritative current state. persistedItems.clearLayers() removes stale areas (covering missed DELETEs). Fresh layers are rendered from the response (covering missed CREATEs and UPDATEs). One REST call handles all missed event types simultaneously.

D. Can REST operations continue while realtime updates are unavailable?
Yes. REST is fully independent of WebSocket state. authInterceptor attaches the current JWT to every HTTP request. All mutation success paths update the UI directly without waiting for WebSocket events.

E. Are backend changes required?
No. The backend STOMP endpoint already supports repeated authenticated connections. Each new CONNECT frame is validated independently by JwtChannelInterceptor.

F. How will an expired JWT avoid an infinite reconnect loop?
beforeConnect reads authService.getToken(). When a REST 401 clears the token from localStorage, the next beforeConnect invocation reads null and calls client.deactivate(), stopping all further reconnect attempts. If the token is valid but the STOMP connection is rejected for another reason, onStompError provides an additional logging and deactivation hook. The authInterceptor then navigates to /auth, destroying the MapComponent and calling disconnect().

